# Line endings: git converts NOTHING. The producer decides, and git stores what it
# wrote.
#
# WHY, because "-text everywhere" looks like giving up and is not.
#
# This tree has two legitimate producers with different conventions, and neither is
# wrong:
#
#   *.ts, *.hmiextproj, Description.json, README.md   authored in Visual Studio  -> CRLF
#   *.js, *.d.ts                                      emitted by tsc             -> LF
#   specs, STATE.md, JSON fixtures, most of tools/    authored here              -> LF
#
# Normalising would break both ends. Forcing CRLF makes every `tsc` emit rewrite
# files that did not change; forcing LF fights the toolchain that opens the .ts.
# And a `text` attribute of any kind would make the index disagree with the 260
# files already committed, so adding one would queue the whole tree for rewriting
# on the next checkout - which is the one thing this must not do.
#
# `-text` also protects against the real hazard, which is not this machine: a
# contributor on Windows has `core.autocrlf=true` by default, and without this file
# git would rewrite every LF file on checkout and every CRLF file on commit. With
# it, the committed bytes are the bytes, everywhere.
#
# The authoring rule that goes with this lives in SubstrateMap-Working-Protocol.md:
# match the file you are editing, and DETECT rather than assume.

*                   -text

# Explicit, so no future `text=auto` heuristic reaches them.
*.ts                -text
*.js                -text
*.d.ts              -text
*.mjs               -text
*.json              -text
*.md                -text
*.xml               -text
*.xsd               -text
*.css               -text
*.html              -text
*.view              -text
*.nuspec            -text
*.hmiextproj        -text
*.sln               -text
# THE ONE EXCEPTION (F-3, D-224). A shell script does not have an author's
# convention, it has an interpreter's requirement: the kernel reads the shebang
# byte for byte, so `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` sends it looking for `bash\r`, and
# `set -u\r` is an invalid option. `-text` faithfully preserved the CRLF that a
# Windows editor wrote into typecheck.sh, and it shipped broken.
#
# The comment above warns that a `text` attribute queues the tree for rewriting.
# That argument is about `*`. This one matches a single tracked file, which this
# task rewrites to LF anyway, so the index and the working tree already agree.
*.sh                text eol=lf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png               binary
*.pdf               binary
*.tar               binary
*.tar.gz            binary
*.gz                binary
*.zip               binary
*.nupkg             binary
